+# The Daily Grind — My Account
+
+A responsive, dependency-free passkey management interface, available at `/` and `/account/`.
+
+## Current setup
+
+The supplied project has no authentication service or database. The page therefore opens in **demo mode**, identified below the account content. The sample passkeys can be renamed or removed, and changes persist in local browser storage. Reset demo restores the sample data. Demo IDs are valid Base64URL strings, but demo data is **never** sent to a real authenticator. No actual passkey registration or authentication is simulated.
+
+## Connect the authenticated backend
+
+Change `data-passkey-mode="demo"` to `data-passkey-mode="api"` on the `<html>` element in both HTML entrypoints. `passkey-store.mjs` then uses these same-origin endpoints:
+
+- `GET /api/credentials` → `{ rpId, userId, user: { displayName }, credentials }`
+- `PUT /api/credential/:id`, JSON `{ name }` → successful 2xx response
+- `DELETE /api/credential/:id` → successful 2xx response
+
+Each credential must have `id`, `name`, `registeredAt` (epoch milliseconds), optional `lastUsedAt` (epoch milliseconds), `provider`, `device` (`laptop`, `phone`, `usb`, or generic key), and optional `current`. Provider labels should be resolved from verified registration metadata, with a generic fallback for unknown/zero AAGUIDs. `current` must come from the authenticated session rather than a user-agent guess. IDs and `userId` must be valid, unpadded Base64URL strings. `rpId` must come from the server's registration configuration; it is never inferred from the hostname. Dates are displayed in UTC.
+
+The backend must authenticate every request, enforce credential ownership, validate names, protect mutations against CSRF (including Origin checks and appropriate session-cookie settings), and return only the signed-in customer's credentials. Serve over HTTPS. Do not enable API mode until these endpoints exist. The UI adapter is not a replacement for server-side authorization.
+
+After successful mutation, the client reloads the authoritative list. It signals all accepted IDs on initial load and after deletion, including deletion of the final key. Browser API support and ID encoding are checked, and sync failures remain nonfatal. Unsupported browsers are given manual password-manager cleanup guidance. Renaming a credential changes only its account nickname; it does not rename the account profile or call `signalCurrentUserDetails`. Actual provider cleanup is browser-controlled and not guaranteed.
+
+## Checks
+
+Run `node --test tests/passkeys.test.mjs` for persistence, API failure, and authenticator synchronization tests. There is no build step. No server or manual browser verification is required for these checks.
+
+Coffee photography: [Unsplash](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1447933601403-0c6688de566e) (served locally from `assets/coffee-beans.jpg`). Typography uses Google Fonts with system fallbacks.
